1
00:00:05,550 --> 00:00:09,175
- Email, and so getting logged onto an email,

2
00:00:09,175 --> 00:00:11,910
they had to, we had to create an email.

3
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:15,390
And then obviously I can't be too mean,

4
00:00:15,390 --> 00:00:18,060
my vice president of technology isn't here,

5
00:00:18,060 --> 00:00:20,340
but, so, but no, he knows.

6
00:00:20,340 --> 00:00:21,173
He knows.

7
00:00:21,173 --> 00:00:23,160
And so the thing is,

8
00:00:23,160 --> 00:00:24,840
once we then moved onto the next step

9
00:00:24,840 --> 00:00:26,160
of logging onto an email,

10
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:28,263
well, we had multifactor authentication,

11
00:00:29,340 --> 00:00:31,800
and at the time we had two of them.

12
00:00:31,800 --> 00:00:34,530
And so we're safer now.

13
00:00:34,530 --> 00:00:36,730
And so this is not a bash towards you, Arla,

14
00:00:37,740 --> 00:00:41,400
but my base is, you know,

15
00:00:41,400 --> 00:00:45,420
it was not an easy process of onboarding students.

16
00:00:45,420 --> 00:00:49,440
And so thinking that we could onboard 14 students

17
00:00:49,440 --> 00:00:51,720
that had never touched a computer before in their life

18
00:00:51,720 --> 00:00:53,730
in an hour and a half period of time,

19
00:00:53,730 --> 00:00:55,080
not gonna happen.

20
00:00:55,080 --> 00:00:58,202
I mean, and I say that genuinely

21
00:00:58,202 --> 00:01:01,202
that we changed the next group

22
00:01:01,202 --> 00:01:03,870
that we onboarded two weeks later.

23
00:01:03,870 --> 00:01:05,730
We did one-on-one.

24
00:01:05,730 --> 00:01:07,983
And so we made changes there.

25
00:01:08,850 --> 00:01:09,990
As we continue to look

26
00:01:09,990 --> 00:01:11,790
at like the development side of things,

27
00:01:11,790 --> 00:01:12,623
of what we're doing

28
00:01:12,623 --> 00:01:14,663
with our competency-based education program,

29
00:01:14,663 --> 00:01:18,480
I mentioned we're the very first competency-based program

30
00:01:18,480 --> 00:01:20,223
in agriculture in California.

31
00:01:21,120 --> 00:01:23,460
There was no one that we could compare to

32
00:01:23,460 --> 00:01:26,130
and specifically like the processes

33
00:01:26,130 --> 00:01:27,420
of what we were doing.

34
00:01:27,420 --> 00:01:30,423
It is a non-credit CBE program currently.

35
00:01:31,449 --> 00:01:33,450
There was no transcripts

36
00:01:33,450 --> 00:01:37,980
that we could really copy off of or use.

37
00:01:37,980 --> 00:01:39,750
So we had to develop transcripts,

38
00:01:39,750 --> 00:01:41,250
and we're still developing transcripts.

39
00:01:41,250 --> 00:01:44,160
We've actually, we have developed a competency-based

40
00:01:44,160 --> 00:01:46,980
education transcript, but ensuring that, you know,

41
00:01:46,980 --> 00:01:50,294
as we look at scalability of programs,

42
00:01:50,294 --> 00:01:53,010
we know some of the students not only will take

43
00:01:53,010 --> 00:01:56,250
the competency-based access system certificate,

44
00:01:56,250 --> 00:02:00,420
but they'll also take potentially a for-credit program.

45
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:03,942
And so ensuring that if students are requesting transcripts,

46
00:02:03,942 --> 00:02:05,902
they don't have to--

47
00:02:05,902 --> 00:02:08,880
we're trying to eliminate one less click for them

48
00:02:08,880 --> 00:02:10,980
so that if they wanna request transcripts,

49
00:02:10,980 --> 00:02:14,040
it spits it all out onto two or three documents.

50
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:15,840
That hasn't been an easy process.

51
00:02:15,840 --> 00:02:18,660
And so when we're looking at some of those barriers

52
00:02:18,660 --> 00:02:20,835
from a systematic side of things,

53
00:02:20,835 --> 00:02:22,740
we're still working on that.

54
00:02:22,740 --> 00:02:26,550
And we're, we meet weekly;

55
00:02:26,550 --> 00:02:30,540
we have several different work groups that are part of that.

56
00:02:30,540 --> 00:02:32,880
When we look at the barriers for students,

57
00:02:32,880 --> 00:02:36,810
that's what this specific program has been all about

58
00:02:36,810 --> 00:02:40,290
was how can we remove barriers for students.

59
00:02:40,290 --> 00:02:43,530
The process of applying to a community college

60
00:02:43,530 --> 00:02:46,140
in California is not simple.

61
00:02:46,140 --> 00:02:49,080
We have a wellbeing program at the college,

62
00:02:49,080 --> 00:02:52,980
and I applied to be a student at Merced College

63
00:02:52,980 --> 00:02:55,770
a couple, a few months ago.

64
00:02:55,770 --> 00:02:57,960
I was kind of lost,

65
00:02:57,960 --> 00:03:01,267
and I kind of sit back and I was like,

66
00:03:01,267 --> 00:03:03,870
"How are my students that have never touched a computer

67
00:03:03,870 --> 00:03:05,940
before gonna get through this?"

68
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,033
And so in some programs,

69
00:03:09,033 --> 00:03:11,310
the ag tech program specifically,

70
00:03:11,310 --> 00:03:15,750
it's very, very one-on-one with our support systems.

71
00:03:15,750 --> 00:03:19,680
We know that's not an option for all students, though.

72
00:03:19,680 --> 00:03:23,370
And so how can we cut down barriers for students?

73
00:03:23,370 --> 00:03:26,550
One other barrier that I wanna share is,

74
00:03:26,550 --> 00:03:30,060
you know, when the students get into our program

75
00:03:30,060 --> 00:03:32,970
and they get through their very first competency,

76
00:03:32,970 --> 00:03:35,130
which is digital literacy,

77
00:03:35,130 --> 00:03:37,800
that's our major stress for some of our students.

78
00:03:37,800 --> 00:03:39,540
And for some of them,

79
00:03:39,540 --> 00:03:41,010
once they get halfway through that,

80
00:03:41,010 --> 00:03:46,010
which, you know, could take roughly six to eight hours--

81
00:03:46,401 --> 00:03:48,870
it could take six to eight hours for folks

82
00:03:48,870 --> 00:03:51,600
that are traditional, know how to use computers.

83
00:03:51,600 --> 00:03:55,380
But for some of these, it takes them two, three months.

84
00:03:55,380 --> 00:03:57,720
After that, they're discouraged

85
00:03:57,720 --> 00:04:00,990
and they said, "Is this how this is gonna be for the rest?"

86
00:04:00,990 --> 00:04:04,260
And so we have support systems built in place.

87
00:04:04,260 --> 00:04:07,050
I have two dedicated student support coordinators

88
00:04:07,050 --> 00:04:09,063
that work with those students.

89
00:04:10,350 --> 00:04:11,730
They're constantly calling.

90
00:04:11,730 --> 00:04:14,190
They're saying, "Hey, I haven't seen that you've done

91
00:04:14,190 --> 00:04:16,290
anything on Canvas for the last two weeks.

92
00:04:16,290 --> 00:04:17,827
What's going on?"

93
00:04:17,827 --> 00:04:20,550
"Sorry, I'm sorry, Ning, I am,

94
00:04:20,550 --> 00:04:22,440
I'm in the middle of almond harvest right now.

95
00:04:22,440 --> 00:04:23,677
I gotta take a step away."

96
00:04:23,677 --> 00:04:24,510
"You got it.

97
00:04:24,510 --> 00:04:26,370
We'll call you in another month or so."

98
00:04:26,370 --> 00:04:28,387
Or we see students that,

99
00:04:28,387 --> 00:04:30,240
"Hey, we see you haven't done anything

100
00:04:30,240 --> 00:04:31,140
in the last couple weeks.

101
00:04:31,140 --> 00:04:32,437
What's going on?"

102
00:04:32,437 --> 00:04:33,750
"Well, I was scared to tell you,

103
00:04:33,750 --> 00:04:35,580
but my computer broke."

104
00:04:35,580 --> 00:04:38,250
And so it allows us,

105
00:04:38,250 --> 00:04:41,190
those support coordinators have really allowed for us

106
00:04:41,190 --> 00:04:44,223
to kind of decrease some of those barriers for the students.

107
00:04:45,390 --> 00:04:46,950
Getting onto the college campus

108
00:04:46,950 --> 00:04:48,690
the very first time is daunting.

109
00:04:48,690 --> 00:04:51,570
And if you go to the wrong building,

110
00:04:51,570 --> 00:04:53,100
you go to the wrong office,

111
00:04:53,100 --> 00:04:54,427
for some of these students, they say,

112
00:04:54,427 --> 00:04:56,449
"Hmm, too much.

113
00:04:56,449 --> 00:04:58,320
I'm gonna go home, not gonna do it."

114
00:04:58,320 --> 00:04:59,820
And so I have student support coordinators

115
00:04:59,820 --> 00:05:03,570
that will meet the students at the local Starbucks

116
00:05:03,570 --> 00:05:04,890
and say, "Hey, follow me in."

117
00:05:04,890 --> 00:05:07,200
And so we've noticed that for a lot of these students.

118
00:05:07,200 --> 00:05:10,238
So the farm laborers students,

119
00:05:10,238 --> 00:05:12,300
they need more and more support.

120
00:05:12,300 --> 00:05:14,700
And so, but once we get them in there

121
00:05:14,700 --> 00:05:16,380
and they get some success,

122
00:05:16,380 --> 00:05:18,933
they get through some of these competencies,

123
00:05:19,920 --> 00:05:21,780
they find it really, really rewarding.

124
00:05:21,780 --> 00:05:23,040
The one other thing is

125
00:05:23,040 --> 00:05:27,240
that our industry partners have fully supported

126
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:30,270
their employees to be going through this program,

127
00:05:30,270 --> 00:05:33,180
in which some of them have guaranteed

128
00:05:33,180 --> 00:05:35,580
a dollar to $2 more an hour raise.

129
00:05:35,580 --> 00:05:39,510
And for some of those students, that's huge.

130
00:05:39,510 --> 00:05:41,430
For other students and other companies,

131
00:05:41,430 --> 00:05:46,343
they've promised them to be on a supervisory role

132
00:05:46,343 --> 00:05:49,320
or a managerial role, which, you know,

133
00:05:49,320 --> 00:05:50,700
that's life-changing.

134
00:05:50,700 --> 00:05:51,840
That's family changing.

135
00:05:51,840 --> 00:05:55,832
And so it's an opportunity for us

136
00:05:55,832 --> 00:05:59,820
and for those companies to really show the support

137
00:05:59,820 --> 00:06:01,117
towards those students and say,

138
00:06:01,117 --> 00:06:05,739
"Hey, you know, we understand that agriculture is changing."

139
00:06:05,739 --> 00:06:07,050
'Cause it is.

140
00:06:07,050 --> 00:06:09,030
I mean, when we look at the costs of labor,

141
00:06:09,030 --> 00:06:11,400
when we talk about water efficiency,

142
00:06:11,400 --> 00:06:12,750
when we talk about all these things,

143
00:06:12,750 --> 00:06:14,779
things are changing.

144
00:06:14,779 --> 00:06:16,860
And as we look at just, you know,

145
00:06:16,860 --> 00:06:19,440
the harvesting equipment where, you know,

146
00:06:19,440 --> 00:06:23,040
15 years ago we were reliant upon laborers

147
00:06:23,040 --> 00:06:25,140
to handpick our, you know,

148
00:06:25,140 --> 00:06:27,480
tomatoes or our bell peppers.

149
00:06:27,480 --> 00:06:30,693
Now as we continue to evolve with ag tech,

150
00:06:31,708 --> 00:06:34,290
we see a lot more equipment that's being involved.

151
00:06:34,290 --> 00:06:36,390
A lot more autonomous equipment.

152
00:06:36,390 --> 00:06:40,867
But what the employers are telling us is,

153
00:06:40,867 --> 00:06:42,990
"We don't have people that can operate that equipment."

154
00:06:42,990 --> 00:06:44,040
Now, you know, we've grown,

155
00:06:44,040 --> 00:06:47,430
we've invested in some of this equipment,

156
00:06:47,430 --> 00:06:48,780
but now what we're finding is

157
00:06:48,780 --> 00:06:51,150
we don't have people that can, you know,

158
00:06:51,150 --> 00:06:52,500
operate that equipment.

159
00:06:52,500 --> 00:06:56,520
And so we're essentially upskilling those farm laborers

160
00:06:56,520 --> 00:06:58,770
that were traditionally on their hands and knees

161
00:06:58,770 --> 00:07:01,080
into now operating, you know,

162
00:07:01,080 --> 00:07:03,840
a harvesting piece of equipment

163
00:07:03,840 --> 00:07:07,020
or being able to sit behind a sorter,

164
00:07:07,020 --> 00:07:10,380
an optical sorter, and be able to, you know,

165
00:07:10,380 --> 00:07:13,200
they're able to be upskilled on something like that.

166
00:07:13,200 --> 00:07:14,520
And it makes them a lot more valuable,

167
00:07:14,520 --> 00:07:17,103
and in most cases, they're gonna make more money.

168
00:07:18,450 --> 00:07:20,760
- I think there's a really important piece

169
00:07:20,760 --> 00:07:21,660
as you were talking

170
00:07:21,660 --> 00:07:23,550
kind of went through my head multiple times.

171
00:07:23,550 --> 00:07:27,060
It's all about working smarter, not harder.

172
00:07:27,060 --> 00:07:28,500
It's not taking the job away;

173
00:07:28,500 --> 00:07:30,930
we're just having to think differently with the work.

174
00:07:30,930 --> 00:07:32,670
And I think in ag we've seen this,

175
00:07:32,670 --> 00:07:34,110
and even in manufacturing,

176
00:07:34,110 --> 00:07:37,500
we've seen it in the way automation's come in;

177
00:07:37,500 --> 00:07:39,390
the equipment we're using is changing.

178
00:07:39,390 --> 00:07:42,150
It may not be changing at the speed of like,

179
00:07:42,150 --> 00:07:45,150
you know, some of the more technical worlds

180
00:07:45,150 --> 00:07:46,710
of like the microchip world.

181
00:07:46,710 --> 00:07:48,570
It's not going as fast as that,

182
00:07:48,570 --> 00:07:49,770
but it is changing,

183
00:07:49,770 --> 00:07:51,780
and the skill sets that are needed,

184
00:07:51,780 --> 00:07:54,810
some of those foundational, are needed there regardless.

185
00:07:54,810 --> 00:07:57,570
And then you need to think about how that technology

186
00:07:57,570 --> 00:07:59,100
is layering on top of it.

187
00:07:59,100 --> 00:08:01,410
Our best mechanics come from our shop floor

188
00:08:01,410 --> 00:08:03,900
because they understand the equipment that they run,

189
00:08:03,900 --> 00:08:07,140
and then they become that qualified mechanic afterwards.

190
00:08:07,140 --> 00:08:09,570
I was laughing about the two-factor authentication

191
00:08:09,570 --> 00:08:12,828
because the other day I was trying to get SAP's Concur

192
00:08:12,828 --> 00:08:14,482
on my phone app working.

193
00:08:14,482 --> 00:08:15,959
You should have seen me,

194
00:08:15,959 --> 00:08:17,790
and I work in technology a lot,

195
00:08:17,790 --> 00:08:19,200
and I was like, "It is not working,

196
00:08:19,200 --> 00:08:20,520
it's not going to my authenticator,

197
00:08:20,520 --> 00:08:22,890
it's not going to this, what am I supposed to do?"

198
00:08:22,890 --> 00:08:24,900
And it's like, "I can't go to my website

199
00:08:24,900 --> 00:08:26,070
'cause I'm not supposed to be on it,

200
00:08:26,070 --> 00:08:27,450
but I need to book a flight."

201
00:08:27,450 --> 00:08:29,040
And it was like, "This is crazy."

202
00:08:29,040 --> 00:08:31,080
And we get that in our everyday life,

203
00:08:31,080 --> 00:08:33,210
and that frustration in our everyday life,

204
00:08:33,210 --> 00:08:34,500
it's one of the biggest things

205
00:08:34,500 --> 00:08:36,630
is about how to help that employee

206
00:08:36,630 --> 00:08:40,290
and that barrier of how do we make it easier for them

207
00:08:40,290 --> 00:08:41,880
as they're accessing this technology?

208
00:08:41,880 --> 00:08:45,030
How do we give them the scary help guides,

209
00:08:45,030 --> 00:08:45,863
I'm gonna call it,

210
00:08:45,863 --> 00:08:47,760
rather than admitting that they don't know it?

211
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:50,400
How do we give them that subtle support?

212
00:08:50,400 --> 00:08:54,480
So, you know, "Where do I get to learn about Excel

213
00:08:54,480 --> 00:08:56,310
and how do a pivot table?

214
00:08:56,310 --> 00:08:58,950
Oh, I can stick it in ChatGPT now, yay.

215
00:08:58,950 --> 00:09:00,840
But how do I do it as well?"

216
00:09:00,840 --> 00:09:02,400
Like, where are those tools

217
00:09:02,400 --> 00:09:04,560
and leading them to those to help?

218
00:09:04,560 --> 00:09:05,940
And that's what we found ourselves

219
00:09:05,940 --> 00:09:07,350
in a learning function thinking about,

220
00:09:07,350 --> 00:09:09,300
like, how do we pepper those in

221
00:09:09,300 --> 00:09:11,767
to those resistors or also just like,

222
00:09:11,767 --> 00:09:13,350
"Hey, this tool has some great ways

223
00:09:13,350 --> 00:09:15,690
to go in there and find out how to..."

224
00:09:15,690 --> 00:09:17,220
It doesn't mean it can do it all for you.

225
00:09:17,220 --> 00:09:19,573
And I think that's what we've seen

226
00:09:19,573 --> 00:09:21,390
as industry changes.

227
00:09:21,390 --> 00:09:22,597
We've also seen the fear of,

228
00:09:22,597 --> 00:09:24,630
"This is not my job, I dunno how to do it,

229
00:09:24,630 --> 00:09:25,767
and this is not what I know,"

230
00:09:25,767 --> 00:09:28,890
and we've had to just support them.

231
00:09:28,890 --> 00:09:30,390
And sometimes it's that handholding

232
00:09:30,390 --> 00:09:33,000
just to like how to turn that computer on,

233
00:09:33,000 --> 00:09:35,280
how to navigate the screen,

234
00:09:35,280 --> 00:09:38,047
or as we're putting in new technology into a line,

235
00:09:38,047 --> 00:09:39,330
"This is not removing a job,

236
00:09:39,330 --> 00:09:41,340
it's gonna kind of make your life easier

237
00:09:41,340 --> 00:09:43,710
or you're gonna be doing less manual work."

238
00:09:43,710 --> 00:09:44,640
That was probably the biggest

239
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:48,900
as we brought in automatic depalletizers and palletizers.

240
00:09:48,900 --> 00:09:53,340
No longer are we manually moving rack stacks of glass;

241
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:54,450
the robots are doing it.

242
00:09:54,450 --> 00:09:57,120
So now we're watching to see for the following.

243
00:09:57,120 --> 00:09:58,530
Now, sometimes the quality defects

244
00:09:58,530 --> 00:10:00,000
don't get caught quite as quickly,

245
00:10:00,000 --> 00:10:00,960
but it's okay,

246
00:10:00,960 --> 00:10:02,820
we still have to then build in those things.

247
00:10:02,820 --> 00:10:03,653
So I think that's it.

248
00:10:03,653 --> 00:10:08,070
It's really about, it's enhancing, it's not removing.

249
00:10:08,070 --> 00:10:09,750
And I think that's the same with AI,

250
00:10:09,750 --> 00:10:11,700
that's the same with, you know,

251
00:10:11,700 --> 00:10:13,110
new technologies coming in.

252
00:10:13,110 --> 00:10:14,760
It's about enhancement

253
00:10:14,760 --> 00:10:16,860
and the foundational thinking still

254
00:10:16,860 --> 00:10:18,870
and the building of critical thinking

255
00:10:18,870 --> 00:10:21,170
when it comes to the more complex sides of it.

256
00:10:22,890 --> 00:10:24,180
- Thank you both so much for sharing.

257
