00:11:48,780 --> 00:11:50,280
I know we talked about, you know,

289
00:11:50,280 --> 00:11:52,920
we work a lot smarter, not harder--

290
00:11:52,920 --> 00:11:55,323
and so I use it every single day.

291
00:11:57,390 --> 00:12:00,240
- Yeah, it's funny,

292
00:12:00,240 --> 00:12:02,640
I used to lead the instructional design team

293
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:04,170
as one of my priorities,

294
00:12:04,170 --> 00:12:07,380
and we talked a lot about utilization of that

295
00:12:07,380 --> 00:12:08,640
when we were designing training

296
00:12:08,640 --> 00:12:11,460
and where do you utilize AI to create it versus not.

297
00:12:11,460 --> 00:12:14,160
And you can create a whole training program in AI,

298
00:12:14,160 --> 00:12:16,320
but you still have to go back in

299
00:12:16,320 --> 00:12:17,580
and make sure and validate.

300
00:12:17,580 --> 00:12:19,230
And I think that's what we found

301
00:12:19,230 --> 00:12:22,470
is we are using it as a foundation,

302
00:12:22,470 --> 00:12:24,303
but it doesn't mean it's the final.

303
00:12:25,710 --> 00:12:27,390
We are using AI voice

304
00:12:27,390 --> 00:12:29,040
because that is so much easier.

305
00:12:29,040 --> 00:12:30,900
It's so less time-consuming.

306
00:12:30,900 --> 00:12:33,060
When you find the AI voice platforms

307
00:12:33,060 --> 00:12:35,280
that work and sound real,

308
00:12:35,280 --> 00:12:37,020
we're like with it, we'll use it.

309
00:12:37,020 --> 00:12:38,880
And there's a couple that we've used for that

310
00:12:38,880 --> 00:12:40,470
where we're like, "We like this AI,"

311
00:12:40,470 --> 00:12:42,930
but we've purchased it for a month to see if we like it.

312
00:12:42,930 --> 00:12:45,030
And then if we like the voices that are coming through

313
00:12:45,030 --> 00:12:46,740
and they're not sounding robotic,

314
00:12:46,740 --> 00:12:48,240
I love not having to record voice.

315
00:12:48,240 --> 00:12:49,260
We'll have to listen to me.

316
00:12:49,260 --> 00:12:50,100
I'm like, "Good.

317
00:12:50,100 --> 00:12:51,510
I don't have to walk past someone's computer

318
00:12:51,510 --> 00:12:53,880
where they're listening and I'm like, ugh, that's me."

319
00:12:53,880 --> 00:12:55,830
So it's great in that side of it.

320
00:12:55,830 --> 00:12:56,790
We've been able to remove,

321
00:12:56,790 --> 00:13:00,180
and it's reduced time for our team members.

322
00:13:00,180 --> 00:13:03,090
We've used it for translation for certain things,

323
00:13:03,090 --> 00:13:04,170
but we've been very careful

324
00:13:04,170 --> 00:13:06,270
'cause we usually want it court-appointed.

325
00:13:06,270 --> 00:13:09,060
But it has helped where we don't need court-appointed.

326
00:13:09,060 --> 00:13:12,000
And again, there's iffy around Spanish translations

327
00:13:12,000 --> 00:13:15,393
and the ag industry Spanish; it's very different.

328
00:13:16,710 --> 00:13:17,670
We use it,

329
00:13:17,670 --> 00:13:21,960
I use it a lot with data to play with data and see

330
00:13:21,960 --> 00:13:24,450
or if I've got multiple documents to summarize,

331
00:13:24,450 --> 00:13:26,220
like, "Can you find me the trends?

332
00:13:26,220 --> 00:13:27,270
Can you find me...?"

333
00:13:27,270 --> 00:13:29,580
Again, I still need to do some reading.

334
00:13:29,580 --> 00:13:31,960
So in my spare time, I guess

335
00:13:31,960 --> 00:13:33,810
I do quite a lot of work for the state

336
00:13:33,810 --> 00:13:34,980
in different areas,

337
00:13:34,980 --> 00:13:37,530
and sometimes I get 150 pages to read,

338
00:13:37,530 --> 00:13:38,640
and sometimes just putting it in

339
00:13:38,640 --> 00:13:42,420
to see what the key themes are is really useful.

340
00:13:42,420 --> 00:13:44,610
The other day I used it to write a marketing campaign.

341
00:13:44,610 --> 00:13:47,310
I used Copilot to write a marketing campaign

342
00:13:47,310 --> 00:13:49,320
for some training courses we created,

343
00:13:49,320 --> 00:13:51,030
and I could literally,

344
00:13:51,030 --> 00:13:52,710
'cause my creative brain was not there,

345
00:13:52,710 --> 00:13:55,740
and I could literally pick the instructor guides.

346
00:13:55,740 --> 00:13:57,720
So the facilitator guides that have the speed,

347
00:13:57,720 --> 00:13:59,700
have the scripts and the timing

348
00:13:59,700 --> 00:14:01,110
and the pictures of the slides,

349
00:14:01,110 --> 00:14:03,420
and ask it to pull out like key learnings

350
00:14:03,420 --> 00:14:06,660
or, "What are key business purposes behind this training?"

351
00:14:06,660 --> 00:14:07,740
Even though I kind of knew

352
00:14:07,740 --> 00:14:10,080
'cause I knew the purpose 'cause it was a project,

353
00:14:10,080 --> 00:14:12,030
but I couldn't get creative in the writing of it.

354
00:14:12,030 --> 00:14:13,530
It pulled pieces out for me

355
00:14:13,530 --> 00:14:15,720
and made me think differently around it.

356
00:14:15,720 --> 00:14:17,520
But it's about how you use it,

357
00:14:17,520 --> 00:14:19,020
and that's how I'm finding it is, like,

358
00:14:19,020 --> 00:14:22,020
I'm using it, but then I have to use my brain against it.

359
00:14:22,020 --> 00:14:23,460
Or I have to--

360
00:14:23,460 --> 00:14:26,010
I had one where the learning management system

361
00:14:26,010 --> 00:14:27,480
was dumping a load of data,

362
00:14:27,480 --> 00:14:30,540
and I was like, "I don't have time to pivot table this

363
00:14:30,540 --> 00:14:33,150
into something pretty or to try and work it out."

364
00:14:33,150 --> 00:14:34,860
We found that if we dump it into Copilot

365
00:14:34,860 --> 00:14:36,870
and give it the correct prompts,

366
00:14:36,870 --> 00:14:39,120
it would summarize all of it, and it was fantastic.

367
00:14:39,120 --> 00:14:40,920
It was about movement data of team members,

368
00:14:40,920 --> 00:14:42,270
and I had 500 team members.

369
00:14:42,270 --> 00:14:43,410
I was looking at movement data of,

370
00:14:43,410 --> 00:14:44,820
it was hurting my head,

371
00:14:44,820 --> 00:14:46,560
and it was about timing, roles,

372
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:49,800
what was their progression from A to B to C.

373
00:14:49,800 --> 00:14:52,320
And that did it for me much quicker

374
00:14:52,320 --> 00:14:54,540
than it would've taken me to do it in another way

375
00:14:54,540 --> 00:14:56,250
'cause it was data that you had to think about,

376
00:14:56,250 --> 00:14:57,960
like, the formatting and all of that.

377
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:01,920
So I don't say I would use it 50% of my time at all,

378
00:15:01,920 --> 00:15:04,230
but I use it in and out as I need to,

379
00:15:04,230 --> 00:15:07,890
and I found I've had to get braver about it,

380
00:15:07,890 --> 00:15:08,730
if that makes sense.

381
00:15:08,730 --> 00:15:09,577
Like I've had to be like,

382
00:15:09,577 --> 00:15:10,980
"I'm just gonna try it

383
00:15:10,980 --> 00:15:12,270
if it doesn't give me anything."

384
00:15:12,270 --> 00:15:13,987
But there are some things where I'm like,

385
00:15:13,987 --> 00:15:15,660
"It doesn't make sense.

386
00:15:15,660 --> 00:15:19,740
It's quicker just to write it than it is to put it in."

387
00:15:19,740 --> 00:15:21,780
Whereas I know some people are putting everything in

388
00:15:21,780 --> 00:15:23,640
and spitting it out and then submitting,

389
00:15:23,640 --> 00:15:25,890
and I'm like, "Oh, I can tell you that's not your writing."

390
00:15:25,890 --> 00:15:27,120
You know, just by the way it reads,

391
00:15:27,120 --> 00:15:28,290
like every educator knows

392
00:15:28,290 --> 00:15:30,157
when they can tell for plagiarism.

393
00:15:30,157 --> 00:15:31,680
- "I hope this email finds you well."

394
00:15:31,680 --> 00:15:32,940
- Yeah, exactly.

395
00:15:32,940 --> 00:15:33,773
Or some of the others.

396
00:15:33,773 --> 00:15:35,490
And I'm like, "Okay, this is..."

397
00:15:35,490 --> 00:15:37,620
But I was writing something last night

398
00:15:37,620 --> 00:15:39,360
and I was like, "I'm not that persuasive.

399
00:15:39,360 --> 00:15:41,130
I wanted to make it persuasive to them.

400
00:15:41,130 --> 00:15:42,660
I wanted them to like recognize something."

401
00:15:42,660 --> 00:15:43,790
I was like, "I'm gonna put it in there."

402
00:15:43,790 --> 00:15:44,820
It was an external thing,

403
00:15:44,820 --> 00:15:47,880
and I was like, "I want them to realize."

404
00:15:47,880 --> 00:15:48,990
And so I put it into chat,

405
00:15:48,990 --> 00:15:50,287
I put it into Copilot and said,

406
00:15:50,287 --> 00:15:51,570
"Summarize it for me."

407
00:15:51,570 --> 00:15:54,390
I love it for meetings and summaries of meetings.

408
00:15:54,390 --> 00:15:56,700
And we've been told, "Don't use Otter.ai."

409
00:15:56,700 --> 00:15:58,860
We used to use Otter.ai for a lot of focus group work

410
00:15:58,860 --> 00:16:00,480
and like facilitation things,

